  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Geomagnetic storms are major disturbances in Earth's magnetosphere caused by solar wind energy transfer, typically from coronal mass ejections (CMEs) or high-speed streams. They can last from hours to days, causing bright aurora, satellite/radio disruptions, and potential power grid issues, with recent severe G4-level activity reported in January 2026. Causes and Mechanisms Coronal Mass Ejections (CMEs): Large clouds of plasma and magnetic fields ejected from the sun, which can take 18 hours to several days to arrive. Solar Wind: High-speed streams (HSS) and co-rotating interaction regions (CIRs) can trigger storms. Magnetic Interaction: The southward directed solar wind magnetic field interacts with Earth’s magnetic field. Effects of a Geomagnetic Storm Auroras: Increased intensity allows auroras to be visible at much lower latitudes than normal, sometimes reaching mid-latitudes. Technology Disruption: Induced currents can overwhelm power grids, leading to transformer overheating and blackouts. Satellite/Radio Impacts: Potential for satellite failures, GPS disruptions, and radio blackouts. Astronaut/Flight Safety: Increased radiation exposure is possible for astronauts and, to a lesser extent, high-altitude air travel. Duration and Classification Duration: Storms can last from a few hours to several days. Stages: They consist of an initial phase (sudden commencement), a main phase (2−8 hours), and a recovery phase (can last up to 7 days). Scale: NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center (.gov) uses a G1-G5 scale, with G4 (Severe) recently observed on Jan 19, 2026, and a G2 (Moderate) watch issued on March 18, 2026. Recent Activity January 2026: A significant solar storm, including a G4-level event on Jan 19, 2026, resulted from an X-class flare. March 2026: A G2 (Moderate) storm watch was issued following CMEs released on March 16.",
